  volume    =  38,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "78--92",
  abstract  = "ABSTRACTThis essay addresses a foundational topic in applied
               sciences with interest in design: how do theories inform design?
               Previous work has attributed theory-use to abduction and
               deduction. However, design is about creating an intervention, a
               possible state that does not exist presently, and these accounts
               fail to explain how theories permit taking this leap. We argue
               that the practical value of a theory lies in counterfactual
               thinking. Theories are like ?speculation pumps?: they produce
               (pump) counterfactual thought experiments of the type: If design
               was , then interaction would be . The more valid these thought
               experiments are and the better they direct the solution of design
               problems toward desirable and reliable outcomes, the more useful
               the theory. Counterfactual thinking sheds new light to design
               methods and, importantly, can reconcile an underlying tension
               between design sciences and applied sciences.",
