  volume    =  45,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "70--85",
  abstract  = "Boundary objects are entities that enhance the capacity of an
               idea, theory or practice to translate across culturally defined
               boundaries, for example, between communities of knowledge or
               practice. This concept thus has potential to both explain and
               predict technology adoption; however, it remains sociologically
               under-theorized. This article assesses, by recourse to a
               historical case study of innovations in surgical sterility, how
               boundary objects work and their relationship to social meanings
               within communities of practice. It is concluded that not only are
               there positive and negative boundary objects, but that
               technological devices or processes may themselves act as
