               framework for DS research and of a template for its presentation
               may have contributed to its slow adoption. The design science
               research methodology (DSRM) presented here incorporates
               principles, practices, and procedures required to carry out such
               research and meets three objectives: it is consistent with prior
               literature, it provides a nominal process model for doing DS
               research, and it provides a mental model for presenting and
               evaluating DS research in IS. The DS process includes six steps:
               problem identification and motivation, definition of the
               objectives for a solution, design and development, demonstration,
               evaluation, and communication. We demonstrate and evaluate the
