                 individual to produce creative work. The theory is grounded in
                 a definition of creativity as the production of ideas or
                 outcomes that are both novel and appropriate to some goal. In
                 this theory, four components are necessary for any creative
                 response: three components within the individual-domainrelevant
                 skills, creativity-relevant processes, and intrinsic task
                 motivation-and one component outside the individual-the social
                 environment in which the individual is working. The current
                 version of the theory encompasses organizational creativity and
                 innovation, carrying implications for the work environments
                 created by managers. This entry defines the components of
                 creativity and how they influence the creative process,
                 describing modifications to the theory over time. Then, after
                 comparing the componential theory to other creativity theories,
                 the article describes this theory's evolution and impact.",
  year        =  2012,
  url         = "https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/12-096.pdf"
}

